WEBVTT

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.940
Hi! I'm Sprout,

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.980
and I love a good graph.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.000
ACT science looks like hard science.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.780
A lot of it is reading charts very carefully.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.700
A table or a graph is just measurements.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.660
So before comparing anything,

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.160
read what's being measured,

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.140
and its units.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:21.860
Here's a table.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.140
Time, in minutes.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:24.740
Temperature, in degrees.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.780
Ten minutes, eighteen degrees.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.540
Twenty minutes, twenty-two.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.560
Thirty minutes, twenty-six.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.980
Question: what's the temperature at twenty minutes?

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:35.500
Don't read everything.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.000
Go straight to the row.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:38.720
Twenty minutes. Across.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:39.620
Twenty-two degrees.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.250
On a graph,

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:43.470
it's the same move.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:45.010
Find twenty on the axis.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.290
Up to the line.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:47.690
Across. Twenty-two.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:51.690
Your turn. This one is between the points.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:53.910
What's the temperature at fifteen minutes,

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:55.210
roughly? Go.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:03.430
About twenty! It's halfway between the points,

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:04.690
and the line is steady.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.770
That's interpolation: inside the data,

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:09.990
and pretty safe.

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:11.330
Going beyond the data,

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:12.450
like at two hours?

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:14.730
That's extrapolation. It's uncertain.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:16.590
The trend might not continue.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.070
And check the legends and axis scales.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:21.490
A broken scale,

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:22.610
or a logarithmic one,

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.790
changes what distance on the graph means.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.430
Variables. Units. Then go straight to what the question

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.170
asks. Now hit Try this skill!
